# Call-site ledger of configargparse at 1829aa14c317374aa1467e1d01f99472282183ff.
# Decide every `undecided` line; see macro-benchmark/README.md.
#
# The scope rules A, B1-B5 and C are in macro-benchmark/README.md;
# configargparse.md shows how this ledger applies them. Supported versions:
# Python 3.6 and later (setup.py:125 python_requires=">=3.6"), so both sides
# of the version branch at line 31 run, and Python 3.6's argparse counts.
# setup.py also explicitly supports PyPy. Include CPython and PyPy >=3.6,
# with no declared upper bound, in normal, -O and -OO modes (round-4
# working configuration assumption; not an explicit user approval).
#
# The module replaces argparse._ActionsContainer.add_argument with its own
# add_argument (line 2447), at import, before any parser exists; the
# original stays reachable as original_add_argument_method (line 2444). So
# self.add_argument(...) on any argparse container, also inside argparse
# (ArgumentParser.__init__ adds -h/--help that way), runs
# configargparse.add_argument.
#
# Values that the package defines for its roles (B2):
# - config file parsers: an instance of any package ConfigFileParser class
#   (ConfigFileParser itself is a plain class, public and instantiable);
# - config streams: io.StringIO (line 1439) and the text file that the
#   default config_file_open_func, the builtin open, returns
#   (io.TextIOWrapper); streams that a client function returns are opaque;
# - items of serialize() and the result of parse(): collections.OrderedDict
#   (the docstrings at lines 113 and 124 fix it, and the package builds one).
#
# Package exception classes define no __init__: instantiating one runs the
# __init__ of Exception, named through Exception, the nearest class outside
# the package (as in pluggy).

== configargparse

